## 1\. Introduction[](#introduction)

*This section is non-normative.*

Operating systems generally allow users to connect multiple screens to a single device and arrange them virtually to extend the overall visual workspace.

A variety of applications use platform tools to place their content in such multi-screen environments, but web application developers are limited by existing APIs, which were generally designed around the use of a single screen.

As multi-screen devices and applications become a more common and critical part of user experiences, it becomes more important to give web developers information and tools to leverage that expanded visual environment.

This specification incrementally extends the `[Window](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/nav-history-apis.html#window)`, `[Screen](https://www.w3.org/TR/cssom-view-1/#screen)`, and `[FullscreenOptions](https://fullscreen.spec.whatwg.org/#dictdef-fullscreenoptions)` APIs, and introduces new `[ScreenDetails](#screendetails)` and `[ScreenDetailed](#screendetailed)` interfaces. These changes allow web applications to offer compelling multi-screen experiences by placing content on specific screens.

### 1.1. Motivating Use Cases[](#motivations)

The aim of this specification is enable better experiences for web application users with multiple screens. Here are some use cases that inform the design:

-   Slideshow app presents on a projector, shows speaker notes on a laptop screen.

-   Financial app opens a dashboard of windows across multiple monitors.

-   Medical app opens images (e.g. x-rays) on a high-resolution grayscale display.

-   Creativity app shows secondary windows (e.g. palette) on a separate screen.

-   Conference room app shows controls on a touch screen device and video on a TV.

-   Multi-screen layouts in gaming, signage, artistic, and other types of apps.

-   Site optimizes content and layout when a window spans multiple screens.

### 1.2. Usage Overview[](#usage-overview)

To support multi-screen experiences, the API allows web applications to:

1.  Detect if the device has more than one screen

2.  Request information required to place content on specific screens

3.  Detect when a screen is added or removed

4.  Detect when the current screen or an attribute thereof changes

5.  Show an element fullscreen on a specific screen

6.  Place a window on a specific screen

7.  Initiate a multi-screen experience from a single transient user activation

A basic example of the API usage is as follows:

// Detect if the device has more than one screen.
if (window.screen.isExtended) {
// Request information required to place content on specific screens.
const screenDetails \= await window.getScreenDetails();

// Detect when a screen is added or removed.
screenDetails.addEventListener('screenschange', onScreensChange);

// Detect when the current \\\`ScreenDetailed\\\` or an attribute thereof changes.
screenDetails.addEventListener('currentscreenchange', onCurrentScreenChange);

// Find the primary screen, show some content fullscreen there.
const primaryScreen \= screenDetails.screens.find(s \=> s.isPrimary);
document.documentElement.requestFullscreen({screen : primaryScreen});

// Find a different screen, fill its available area with a new window.
const otherScreen \= screenDetails.screens.find(s \=> s !== primaryScreen);
window.open(url, '\_blank', \\\`left=${otherScreen.availLeft},\\\` +
\\\`top=${otherScreen.availTop},\\\` +
\\\`width=${otherScreen.availWidth},\\\` +
\\\`height=${otherScreen.availHeight}\\\`);
} else {
// Detect when an attribute of the legacy \\\`Screen\\\` interface changes.
window.screen.addEventListener('change', onScreenChange);

// Arrange content within the traditional single-screen environment...
}

#### 1.2.1. Detect the presence of multiple screens[](#usage-overview-screen-extended)

A principal question for supporting multi-screen experiences is whether the device has multiple screens that can be used when placing content. These screens can be built-in to the device (like a laptop display panel), attached to the device by wire (like a computer and monitor attached by an HDMI cable), connected to the device by other means (like Mac and iPad Sidecar functionality), or through display device virtualization. This is provided by the `[isExtended](#dom-screen-isextended)` boolean, exposed to secure contexts without a permission prompt.

if (screen.isExtended) {
// Offer multi-screen controls for the user.
}

#### 1.2.2. Detect `[Screen](https://www.w3.org/TR/cssom-view-1/#screen)` attribute changes[](#usage-overview-screen-changes)

Observing legacy `[Screen](https://www.w3.org/TR/cssom-view-1/#screen)` attribute changes is useful for adapting content, even on single-screen devices. Furthermore, observing `[isExtended](#dom-screen-isextended)` is useful for detecting transitions between single-screen and multi-screen configurations. To avoid polling, `[change](#eventdef-screen-change)` events are fired at the `[Screen](https://www.w3.org/TR/cssom-view-1/#screen)` object:

screen.addEventListener('change', e \=> {
// An attribute of the legacy \\\`Screen\\\` interface has changed.
});

#### 1.2.3. Request detailed screen information[](#usage-overview-screen-details)

Detailed information about screens used by the device can be requested through the `[getScreenDetails()](#dom-window-getscreendetails)` method. This method may prompt the user for permission. The resulting `[ScreenDetails](#screendetails)` object lets developers enumerate screens, inspect their attributes, and listen for changes.

try {
// Request screen details and process the information immediately.
const screenDetails \= await window.getScreenDetails();
processScreenDetails(screenDetails);

// Process updated screen details when the set of screens change.
screenDetails.onscreenschange \= () \=> {
processScreenDetails(screenDetails);
};
} catch (err) {
console.error(err);
// Handle denied permission and other errors.
}

function processScreenDetails(screenDetails) {
// Build a UI listing screens, using assumed helpers.
clearScreenList();
screenDetails.screens.forEach(screen \=> {
addToScreenList({name: screen.label, screen: screen});
// Process updated screen details when a particular screen's details change.
screen.onchange \= () \=> {
processScreenDetails(screenDetails);
};
});
selectCurrentInScreenList(screenDetails.currentScreen);
}

#### 1.2.4. Place fullscreen content on a specific screen[](#usage-overview-place-fullscreen-content-on-a-specific-screen)

A common multi-screen use case is to present some content fullscreen on a specific screen. The screen may be selected interactively, or automatically selected based on screen attributes or prior user selections. A screen, once selected, can be passed to the `[requestFullscreen()](https://fullscreen.spec.whatwg.org/#dom-element-requestfullscreen)` method.

// Call an assumed helper that returns a selected \\\`ScreenDetailed\\\` instance.
const screenDetailed \= getScreenForSlideshow();

// Request that a particular element be shown fullscreen on the selected screen.
slideshowElement.requestFullscreen({ screen: screenDetailed });

#### 1.2.5. Place windows on a specific screen[](#usage-overview-place-windows-on-a-specific-screen)

Another common multi-screen use case is to place a window on a specific screen. This can be accomplished by using coordinates provided in the `[ScreenDetailed](#screendetailed)` interface with existing `[open()](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/nav-history-apis.html#dom-open)` and `[moveTo()](https://www.w3.org/TR/cssom-view-1/#dom-window-moveto)` methods.

function openCenteredWindow(url, screenDetailed, w, h) {
// Compute coordinates centering the window on the target screen.
const l \= screenDetailed.left + Math.round(screenDetailed.width \- w) / 2;
const t \= screenDetailed.top + Math.round(screenDetailed.height \- h) / 2;

// Open the window with the requested dimensions.
return window.open(url, '\_blank', \\\`left=${l},top=${t},width=${w},height=${h}\\\`);
}

#### 1.2.6. Initiate multi-screen experiences[](#usage-overview-initiate-multi-screen-experiences)

A commonly requested multi-screen use case is to initiate a compelling multi-screen experience from a single user activation. One specific proposed form is permitting a site to [§ 1.2.4 Place fullscreen content on a specific screen](#usage-overview-place-fullscreen-content-on-a-specific-screen) and [§ 1.2.5 Place windows on a specific screen](#usage-overview-place-windows-on-a-specific-screen) from a single user gesture. This can be accomplished by first requesting fullscreen on a specific screen of a multi-screen device, and then opening a popup window on another screen of the device, in a single event listener.

initiateMultiScreenExperienceButton.addEventListener('click', async () \=> {
// Find the primary screen, show some content fullscreen there.
const primaryScreen \= screenDetails.screens.find(s \=> s.isPrimary);
await document.documentElement.requestFullscreen({screen : primaryScreen});

// Find a different screen, fill its available area with a new window.
const otherScreen \= screenDetails.screens.find(s \=> s !== primaryScreen);
window.open(url, '\_blank', \\\`left=${otherScreen.availLeft},\\\` +
\\\`top=${otherScreen.availTop},\\\` +
\\\`width=${otherScreen.availWidth},\\\` +
\\\`height=${otherScreen.availHeight}\\\`);
});

## 2\. Concepts[](#concepts)

Concepts in this specification build upon those in the [CSSOM-View-1 Working Draft](https://www.w3.org/TR/cssom-view-1/), and the [CSSOM-View-1 Editor’s Draft](https://drafts.csswg.org/cssom-view/), [\[HTML\]](#biblio-html "HTML Standard"), and [\[Fullscreen\]](#biblio-fullscreen "Fullscreen API Standard").

### 2.1. Screen[](#concept-screen)

A device hosting the user agent has a single screen or multiple [screens](#screen), which are used to display visual content. The set of [screens](#screen) used by a device may change while a user agent is running, reflecting changes in the hardware or software configuration of the device.

Note: Some basic examples of screen configuration changes include connecting a television or projector to a laptop with an HDMI cable, closing the lid of a laptop to disable its built-in LCD panel, and changing the display resolution of a connected LCD computer monitor.

A [screen](#screen) has a color depth, which is the [color depth](#pixel-color-depth) of the screen’s pixels.

A [screen](#screen) has a device pixel ratio, similar to `[Window](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/nav-history-apis.html#window)`’s `[devicePixelRatio](https://www.w3.org/TR/cssom-view-1/#dom-window-devicepixelratio)`, which is the result of the following algorithm:

1.  Let CSS pixel size be the size of a [CSS pixel](https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-4/#px).

2.  Let device pixel size be the vertical size of a [pixel](#screen-pixels).

3.  Return the result of dividing CSS pixel size by device pixel size.

A [screen](#screen) has an orientation, which is described in [\[screen-orientation\]](#biblio-screen-orientation "Screen Orientation").

A [screen](#screen) has a label, which is a string that meaningfully describes the screen to a user to help them identify and differentiate screens.

Note: The [label](#screen-label) can be an arbitrary string selected by the user agent. It could describe the screen relative to the device, e.g. `"internal"` vs. `"external"`, it could include the dimensions, e.g. `"640×480"`, it could include hardware model information, e.g. `"Acme Telletube 1000x"` from [VESA E-EDID](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Extended_Display_Identification_Data) data, it could include a distinguishing number, e.g. `"screen 1"` vs. `"screen 2"`, or all of the preceding. The label can be an empty string if underlying display details are unknown or the user agent chooses to hide that information. Applications can’t assume that the label contains any specific information, such as the device type, model, dimensions, density, etc.

**While many screen attributes could be used for [active fingerprinting](https://www.w3.org/TR/fingerprinting-guidance/#dfn-active-fingerprinting), the strings used as [labels](#screen-label) in particular should be considered carefully to minimize the uniqueness. For example, it would be a poor choice to include the serial number of the device.**

[](#issue-702b01cb)Should [device pixel ratio](#screen-device-pixel-ratio) include the [page-zoom](https://drafts.csswg.org/cssom-view-1/#page-zoom)?

### 2.2. Screen pixel[](#concept-screen-pixel)

A [screen](#screen) has pixels, which are the smallest screen components that can be programmed directly. Each [pixel](#screen-pixels) shows one color.

Note: On a liquid-crystal display (LCD), each pixel is made up of three components. Each component is a (red, green, blue) light with variable intensity. Reasoning about pixel components (subpixel rendering) is out of scope for this specification.

Note: Some screens can be configured to display content at a resolution that differs from the physical hardware’s inherent composition; e.g. a monitor with a hardware resolution of 2560×1440 can be configured by the device to operate with a display resolution of 1920×1080.

A [pixel](#screen-pixels) has a color depth, which is the number of bits used to represent the colors it can display.

Note: Some popular rendering systems model [pixels](#screen-pixels) as having a [color depth](#pixel-color-depth) of 24. These 3 groups of 8 bits represent the intensity of the (red, green, blue) subpixels of an LCD [pixel](#screen-pixels).

### 2.3. Screen area[](#concept-screen-area)

A [screen](#screen) has a screen area, which is a rectangular two-dimensional grid of [pixels](#screen-pixels), used to present visual content from the operating system and client applications to the user. This corresponds to the [Web-exposed screen area](https://www.w3.org/TR/cssom-view-1/#web-exposed-screen-area) of a particular [screen](#screen).

A [screen area](#screen-screen-area) has a width, which is the number of [pixels](#screen-pixels) along the horizontal dimension of the [screen area](#screen-screen-area)’s rectangular pixel grid.

A [screen area](#screen-screen-area) has a height, which is the number of [pixels](#screen-pixels) along the vertical dimension of the [screen area](#screen-screen-area)’s rectangular pixel grid.

Note: The grid size is usually expressed as <[width](#screen-width)\>×<[height](#screen-height)\>. For example, a 1920×1080 screen area has a grid with a [width](#screen-width) of 1920 pixels and a [height](#screen-height) of 1080 pixels.

Note: As specified in [CSSOM View § 2.3 Web-exposed screen information](https://www.w3.org/TR/cssom-view-1/#web-exposed-screen-information), user agents may choose to hide information about the screen of the output device in order to protect the user’s privacy. In this case, the [screen area](#screen-screen-area) may be equal to that of the [viewport](https://drafts.csswg.org/css2/#viewport).

### 2.4. Available screen area[](#concept-available-screen-area)

A [screen](#screen) has an available screen area, which is a rectangular subset of the [screen area](#screen-screen-area) where the operating system permits web application window placements. The rectangle’s edges are parallel to the [screen area](#screen-screen-area) edges. This region does not include any portions of the [screen area](#screen-screen-area) that are reserved by the operating system for its own user interface elements, such as taskbars and menu bars. This equates to the [Web-exposed available screen area](https://www.w3.org/TR/cssom-view-1/#web-exposed-available-screen-area) of a particular [screen](#screen).

The available width of a [screen](#screen) is the number of [pixels](#screen-pixels) along the horizontal dimension of the [available screen area](#screen-available-screen-area)’s rectangular pixel grid.

The available height of a [screen](#screen) is the number of [pixels](#screen-pixels) along the vertical dimension of the [available screen area](#screen-available-screen-area)’s rectangular pixel grid.

### 2.5. Virtual screen arrangement[](#concept-virtual-screen-arrangement)

A device has a virtual screen arrangement that defines the relative placement of [screens](#screen) that comprise the overall visual environment of the device. The arrangement is commonly composed on a two-dimensional plane extending in all directions, with (x, y)-coordinates that increase rightwards and downwards respectively from the multi-screen origin. The [multi-screen origin](#multi-screen-origin) is an implementation-defined point that defines the (0, 0) coordinate of the [virtual screen arrangement](#virtual-screen-arrangement).

A common convention is to set the [multi-screen origin](#multi-screen-origin) to the top-left of the [primary](#primary) [screen](#screen), but it can be any arbitrary point within the [virtual screen arrangement](#virtual-screen-arrangement). Every [screen](#screen)’s [screen area](#screen-screen-area) is a view onto a rectangular subset of the [virtual screen arrangement](#virtual-screen-arrangement).

This diagram shows some examples of how multiple [screens](#screen) might be arranged within a [virtual screen arrangement](#virtual-screen-arrangement), along with some potential [multi-screen origins](#multi-screen-origin):

![Diagram showing various examples of screens and multi-screen origins](https://www.w3.org/TR/window-management/multi_screen_origin.svg)

Note: The [Second Screen Community Group](https://www.w3.org/community/webscreens/)’s [Form Factors Explained](https://webscreens.github.io/form-factors/) draft report explores related terminology and conceptual models.

### 2.6. Screen position[](#concept-screen-position)

A [screen](#screen) has a screen position which is the [screen area](#screen-screen-area)’s (x, y)-coordinates in the [virtual screen arrangement](#virtual-screen-arrangement) relative to the [multi-screen origin](#multi-screen-origin). Coordinates may be negative, and are typically expressed as (x, y).

### 2.7. Available screen position[](#concept-available-screen-position)

A [screen](#screen) has an available screen position which is the [available screen area](#screen-available-screen-area)’s (x, y)-coordinates in the [virtual screen arrangement](#virtual-screen-arrangement) relative to the [multi-screen origin](#multi-screen-origin). Coordinates may be negative, and are typically expressed as (x, y).

### 2.8. Primary screen[](#concept-primary-screen)

The device hosting the user agent has exactly one primary [screen](#screen). All the other [screens](#screen) are considered secondary.

Note: The primary screen typically hosts the operating system’s user interface for task management, such as the Windows taskbar and the macOS Dock.

A [screen](#screen)’s designation as [primary](#primary) or [secondary](#secondary) may change while the user agent is running.

Note: Most operating systems let the user choose the primary screen using a management user interface, such as the Windows Control Panel and the macOS Preferences application.

### 2.9. Internal screen[](#concept-internal-screen)

Each [screen](#screen) may be designated as internal or external.

[External](#external) screens are manufactured separately from devices that provide their visual output. It is not unusual for an [external](#external) screen to be disconnected from one device and connected to a different device.

Note: As an example, a desktop computer might display its visual output on an [external](#external) screen connected by an HDMI cable. The HDMI cable can be connected or disconnected while the computer is in use, and the computer will adapt its visual environment to that hardware configuration change.

[Internal](#internal) screens are usually attached to a device at manufacturing time. [Internal](#internal) screens are not intended to be detached by users. However an [internal](#internal) [screen](#screen) can still be enabled or disabled while the user agent is running.

Note: As an example, a laptop might disable its [internal](#internal) screen and input device when the lid is closed. The laptop can still be used in this configuration with an [external](#external) screen and input device. The disabled [internal](#internal) screen can not be reported as a [screen](#screen) used by the device while the lid is closed.

### 2.10. Current screen[](#concept-current-screen)

Scripts executing in a `[Window](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/nav-history-apis.html#window)` context can access the `[screen](https://www.w3.org/TR/cssom-view-1/#dom-window-screen)` attribute. That `[Screen](https://www.w3.org/TR/cssom-view-1/#screen)` object reflects properties of the current screen, which is the [screen](#screen) that is currently presenting the window.

Note: On many operating systems, a window can be presented across multiple screens with different properties, or can be in a "hidden" state and not presented on any screen. Operating systems and user agents are assumed to define a canonical screen for a given `[Window](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/nav-history-apis.html#window)`, for example the screen with the largest area intersecting the window.

### 2.11. Observable screen properties[](#concept-observable-screen-properties)

The basic observable properties of a [screen](#screen) are:

-   The [width](#screen-width) and [height](#screen-height) of the [screen area](#screen-screen-area)

-   The [available width](#screen-available-width) and [available height](#screen-available-height) of the [available screen area](#screen-available-screen-area)

-   [color depth](#screen-color-depth)

-   [orientation](#screen-orientation)

The advanced observable properties of a [screen](#screen) are:

-   [screen position](#screen-screen-position)

-   [available screen position](#screen-available-screen-position)

-   [device pixel ratio](#screen-device-pixel-ratio)

-   [label](#screen-label)

-   The [screen](#screen)’s designation as [primary](#primary) or [secondary](#secondary)

-   The [screen](#screen)’s designation as [internal](#internal) or [external](#external)

### 2.12. Window display state[](#concept-window-display-state)

A [top-level browsing context](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/document-sequences.html#top-level-browsing-context) has an associated display state, which can be one of:

normal

The window is in a normal state (neither minimized, maximized, nor fullscreen).

minimized

The window is minimized.

maximized

The window is maximized.

fullscreen

The window is in fullscreen mode.

